# Copied from workflows/web/.env.example — changes: JFlow names; only the test stack is deployed, so the prod line says so and the example points at the local API
# Copy to .env.local. Never commit a real file.

# Where the API lives — an OVERRIDE, not a requirement once the stacks exist. The default is
# resolved at runtime in src/config/env.ts from the hostname, the same pattern as ShipLine,
# JFPRO and Workflows: a hostname in PRODUCTION_HOSTS gets the production stack; localhost,
# Vercel previews and anything unrecognised get the TEST stack.
#
# Three environments, and the stage name IS the environment name; no dev->prod alias map.
#
#   dev    http://localhost:5000/api/v1   (see PORT below; never deployed)
#   test   https://d3votdaxd9.execute-api.eu-north-1.amazonaws.com/api/v1   (the default)
#   prod   not deployed yet. Until it is, PRODUCTION_HOSTS is empty, so every host,
#          jflow.built-form.co.uk included, runs on test and says so.
#
# With no override, every build points at the test stack. Uncomment the line below only to
# run against a local API.
#
# dev and test share one database, so a record made locally is the one the test stage sees.
#
# A localhost address also decides the SIGN-IN GATE: that server bypasses auth and resolves
# everyone as local@dev, so the gate is skipped. Anything else asks for a Google sign-in and
# sends the token as `Authorization: Bearer …`. The gate is therefore ON by default — it
# only switches off when you explicitly point at a local API below.
#
# Vite reads this file ONCE, at startup. Editing it while `npm run dev` is running changes
# nothing — restart.
#VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:5000/api/v1

# Force the environment regardless of hostname ("production" or "test"). Escape hatch for
# serving production from a host not yet listed in src/config/env.ts.
#VITE_APP_ENV=test

# Running the API locally: port 3000 is taken on this machine, so start it on another one.
# The API reads PORT, so nothing in api/ needs editing:
#
#   cd api && PORT=5000 npm run dev                 # Git Bash
#   cd api; $env:PORT=5000; npm run dev             # PowerShell
#
# then set VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:5000/api/v1 above.

# The dev server port. It must be an Authorized JavaScript Origin on the Google OAuth
# client, or the sign-in button fails with origin_mismatch (irrelevant against a localhost
# API, which skips sign-in).
VITE_DEV_PORT=5180
